A||N||6||LemonSky||Help! My Fiero is cranking but not starting!||||9||
Z||000000||LemonSky||07-14-2000||10:00 PM||||Hello, I drive a Gold 1986 Fiero SE6 that is cranking but not starting.<P>First, some history. Last Friday, my battery died, but someone "jumped" me off, and the car started immediately. <P>The next day, I was on my way to purchase a new battery when the car died while at a stop light. I walked down the street and purchased a new battery, installed it, then drove away.<P>However on my way home, the car stalled, but I turned my key and it started immediately. I knew it had to be taken to a garage, but it was Saturday morning, and I had a lot of stuff to do last weekend. <P>So, as I was leaving my home, the Fiero would initially crank but not start. After about thirty seconds, it started. While doing my errands throughout the day, I noticed that it was becoming progressively more difficult to start. And it would continue to stall at stop lights. I'd continue to restart it, but I would prevent it from stalling by throwing it in neutral and keeping it "revved" up.<P>I eventually went home and did some investigating on the internet. I concluded, based on some sites I read, that the problem may be the ignition module. The next day, my car started immediately, and I went to purchase an ignition module which I eventually installed. The car started immediately but it once again stalled while at a stop light. <P>I eventually pulled into a parking lot where unfortunately, it died. It cranked but wouldn't start. And it's been like that since.<P>I had to work this week, so I couldn't get to fixing it, but now I think I may have to take it to a repair shop. I'm reluctant to do this. Is there any advice you can give me? I'm thinking it may be the ignition switch, and if so, is that hard to install?<P>Thanks!||||reg||9
Z||000001||My7Fieros||07-14-2000||10:50 PM||||Prob. one of three things....<BR>1.) Timing set too low<BR>2.) Fuel delivery prob.<BR>3.) "Spark" prob.<BR> Id check for a spark before trying anything else...if no spark (or a week spark) Id go on and replace the ignition coil...that was the prob. in my case. It gets old and corroded, therefore not making a good spark.<BR>My car did the same thing..I tracked it down to a weak/no spark. It finally did like yours and died, not to restart.<BR>I eventually replaced most of the ignition components. <BR>New battery, plugs, wires, and "rebuilt" for lack of a better term, the distributor.<BR>Pull out the distributor, dismantle, clean, and put in a new cap, rotor, and pick up coil (you already replaced the module). That'll usually fix the prob., and youll have to set the timing again, since pulling the dist. out. I'd be willing to bet thats your prob., replacing the pick up coil...if not, then its prob. fuel related, in which case I have no idea what to do....anyone else that can help w/ that area?||||reg||1
Z||000002||BroCar 87||07-14-2000||10:54 PM||||LemonSky, FIRST OFF, WELCOME! <BR>Secondly, I also have a problem like that. JUST last night, it died. I went to go out cruisn'  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">, it started and then died as I was backing out. It died earlier that day, but I managed to get it home. I am having a guy look at it A.S.A.P.! I will tell you the results of his findings, they may be the same as yours. HOPE YOU GET YOURS FIXED.<BR>BroCar 87||||reg||1
Z||000003||84 Indy||07-14-2000||11:10 PM||||This seems to be a common problem.  I ran into this in my 84 SE after replacing every thing including the ECM i still had the problem. It turned out to be a weak fusible link. Two weeks ago my 86 GT did the same thing i changed the fusible links and so far she's been fine    GOOD LUCK AND DON'T GET DISCOURGED||||reg||1
Z||000004||webbee||07-14-2000||11:11 PM||||I'd bet on an electrical problem. I had a coil that would run well if you could get the car started but you couldn't get it to start with out pushing. It started w/intermitent hard starting. Could be the ignition module too.<P>It could be the fuel filter or the fuel pump.<P>Check the spark from the center of the coil if it's hot white/blue then the ignition is ok to that point. Check wires with an ohm meter, or to a grounded spark plug. Plugs are replacable for about a buck each.<P>If you already know all this, sorry for the review.<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||LemonSky||07-14-2000||11:45 PM||||Thanks so far! BTW, the car won't start even if you push it. Hopefully, this will assist some of you in figuring out what my problem may be.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bugsy||07-15-2000||10:38 AM||||a good way to find out if its the ignition coil is to jump it, a weak coil will usually start the engine when you're throwing 24 volts at it<BR>just my .02  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Kevin||||reg||1
